# Line endings.
#
# Without this file, a clone onto Windows checks every LF file out as CRLF and
# git then reports the whole repository as modified — 292 files and ~50,000
# changed lines where nothing was actually edited. That makes `git status`
# useless for seeing what you did, and a commit made in that state touches
# hundreds of files, so `git log` can never answer "when did this break?".
#
# `text=auto` normalises everything to LF in the repository while leaving each
# working tree alone. It is checked in rather than left to `core.autocrlf`
# because a per-machine setting is one every new developer has to remember.
* text=auto

# Explicitly LF — these are read by tooling that does not tolerate CRLF, and a
# shell script with CRLF line endings fails with a confusing `bad interpreter`.
*.ts     text eol=lf
*.tsx    text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.sql    text eol=lf
*.prisma text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Windows-only files keep CRLF.
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
